WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.920
Hi, Steve Kaufman here.

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:04.590
Today I'm going to answer the question.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.590
How do you become good at
speaking in a language?

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.670
What's the secret?

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.710
And I'm going to share with you
the secrets about getting started

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.740
speaking in a language and how
you continue to get better and

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.750
better at speaking in a language.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.440
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.279
And by all means, join me at LingQ,
which is  where I leare languages.

00:00:26.339 --> 00:00:29.279
Now, you have all heard
me talk about input.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.570
I stress input.

00:00:30.959 --> 00:00:33.420
I say you have to get the language in you.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.310
You have to acquire words.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.860
You have to let your brain
get used to the language.

00:00:37.949 --> 00:00:41.879
And that's how, that's where I spend
most of my time, but I do want to speak.

00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:43.200
We all want to speak.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.080
That's the goal for most of
us of learning a language.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.485
We want to communicate with
people, to connect with people.

00:00:49.485 --> 00:00:51.944
We want to express ourselves
in the new language.

00:00:52.035 --> 00:00:53.504
So how do we do it?

00:00:54.044 --> 00:00:57.964
And people have asked me sort of
specifically to go through sort of

00:00:57.964 --> 00:01:03.455
step-by-step because I know that many
of us, and myself included, after an

00:01:03.455 --> 00:01:08.610
extended period of input, of listening
and reading and acquiring words and

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:14.520
seeing my comprehension, you know, improve
and I understand more and more stuff.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.600
And then, you know, I schedule a
session with a tutor and of course

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.310
I'm concerned like, how am I going
to be able to retrieve these words?

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:23.520
You know?

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.330
Oh, by the way, I'm wearing
my t-shirt from Machu Pichu.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.450
And it relates a little bit to
my story and I'll get to that.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.590
At any rate, you know what
happens when we start speaking?

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:35.730
How do we get started?

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:40.020
And that's what I want to kind of cover
in this video, sort of getting started,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.040
speaking in a language and then doing all
the things you need to do so that you can

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:50.670
continue to get better and better, even as
you continue all of your input activities,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.460
even as you continue to acquire words
and phrases and, and, and, um, get

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.080
your brain used to the new language.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.770
So you all know that I spent
most of my time on LingQ.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.360
On LingQ of course we have
all of our input activities.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.800
We also have tutors.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.090
We don't have as many tutors as
italki, but we do have some, and the

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:17.200
advantage at LingQ is that the tutor's
report is incorporated into LingQ.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.580
So I have a record, all of,
of, all of my conversations

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.760
with my tutors now in Persian.

00:02:24.359 --> 00:02:25.320
I'll give you an example.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.890
So Persian and Arabic, both
very difficult because the

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.450
writing system is so difficult.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.549
So what I did in order to break down that
first barrier, in other words, I have

00:02:35.549 --> 00:02:38.100
never said anything in Persian or Arabic.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.510
How do I get started?

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.829
How do I get words out?

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.670
Now, there are people who say
that you can talk to yourself and

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.350
you can say phrases to yourself.

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:49.815
But I've never found that I'm
able to do that for very long.

00:02:49.815 --> 00:02:53.234
I might try it, but I won't
spend a lot of time doing it.

00:02:53.234 --> 00:02:56.144
It's just something that I
have never been able to do.

00:02:56.204 --> 00:02:58.424
People who can do it, good for them.

00:02:58.424 --> 00:03:02.325
It's a way for them to prime
the pump, to get started.

00:03:02.625 --> 00:03:08.685
What I found and what I recommend, what
I found very useful was in languages

00:03:08.685 --> 00:03:13.274
like Persian and Arabic, especially,
but probably in all languages,

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:21.720
I get a tutor, whether on LingQ or
italki, and I say, I want to read and

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.359
what I, and I, I want to then talk about
what I have read and what I use as the

00:03:26.359 --> 00:03:31.100
sort of material that's going to prime
the pump and get me going is the mini

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.899
stories, because the mini stories have
so much repetition built into each story.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.459
And I end up listening to each
one of them, 10, 20, 30 times, not

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:44.980
necessarily at one sitting, but over
the course of a couple of months.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.459
I will have lesson two, lesson one, two,
three, four, five, back to lesson one.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:53.859
And so I'm familiar with what's there and
in each mini story, there are questions,

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:58.150
but the questions are structured in such
a way that you are given the answer.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.680
In other words, a statement, you are
asked the question about that statement

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.130
and then you're given the answer.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.290
So you don't even have
to provide the answer.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.430
It's just more exposure.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.740
But now with my tutor, I am asked
those questions and I have to produce

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:18.149
the answer, but I am producing an
answer that I already know, that I

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:19.950
have listened to many, many times.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:24.915
So, the big thing about
speaking is to reduce stress.

00:04:25.035 --> 00:04:28.635
In fact, if there's one
key it's to reduce stress.

00:04:28.845 --> 00:04:33.405
Stephen Krashen says that we learn
languages when there is meaningful

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:39.405
communication in a low stress or
unstressful environment or situation.

00:04:39.675 --> 00:04:43.365
So anything you can do to reduce
stress is going to help you.

00:04:43.875 --> 00:04:50.055
So, first of all, if you have a tutor,
you're with someone who is paid to

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:55.230
speak to you, so that's less stressful
than hoping that some random person you

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.950
meet is going to want to humor you in
your, in the language you're learning.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:00.570
So that reduces stress.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:05.040
Even amongst the tutors, some
are more sympathetic than others.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.970
Some make a face if you say
something wrong or show impatience,

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.140
you don't want those tutors.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.890
You want the tutor that's very
encouraging, doesn't correct

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:17.610
you all the time, uh, and
keeps the conversation going.

00:05:18.495 --> 00:05:25.575
But also by sort of having a pseudo
conversation where I'm asked questions

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:31.485
that I have heard before and where I have
in fact read the story with the tutor and

00:05:31.485 --> 00:05:34.005
maybe even read some of the questions,

00:05:34.245 --> 00:05:37.365
and then I'm asked the
question, I have the answer.

00:05:37.575 --> 00:05:40.575
I'm not asked to remember
something about the story.

00:05:40.995 --> 00:05:43.775
I'm just asked to say something
that I've heard before and.

00:05:44.505 --> 00:05:45.975
You know, it's very familiar to me.

00:05:46.035 --> 00:05:47.655
I'm likely to succeed.

00:05:47.925 --> 00:05:50.775
Whenever you're given something
to do where you're likely

00:05:50.775 --> 00:05:52.665
to succeed, that's good.

00:05:53.025 --> 00:05:56.025
The brain likes it, likes success.

00:05:56.265 --> 00:05:57.465
Doesn't like failure.

00:05:57.675 --> 00:06:03.075
So I find that beginning to speak in
the language, uh, reading and then

00:06:03.075 --> 00:06:09.539
discussing, using the questions that are
in the many stories is extremely helpful.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.430
And so I would recommend that as a sort
of step number one, where you, you got to

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:18.960
break the ice, you've got to go from all
of your listening and reading to, to a

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.700
situation where you're starting to speak.

00:06:21.179 --> 00:06:25.800
And what I found in both Arabic and
Persian was that I would do that for

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.960
three or four or five, six lessons.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.850
And then I was tired of doing that.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:33.270
Now I wanted to, even within
the 60 minutes, I might do

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:34.590
that for the first 20 minutes.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.880
And then I would start to wander off
into something else, perhaps using

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.530
the vocabulary that I had learned.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.470
So it's, I'm sliding into sort of a
broader range of, you know, exchange with

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.740
my tutor all in a low stress environment.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:51.360
So that's extremely important.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:56.219
The next thing is obviously, when
you start to speak, part of the

00:06:56.219 --> 00:06:59.520
stress comes from your inability
to say what you want to say.

00:06:59.849 --> 00:07:03.719
Part of it comes from not understanding
what the other person is saying,

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:07.050
because you're not good enough
in the language to have a really

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.330
good, free-flowing conversation.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:10.950
Bit of advice.

00:07:11.159 --> 00:07:14.010
Don't worry about what you can't say.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.620
Don't worry about what
you don't understand.

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:20.310
And there is a bit of a
tendency for people to wonder

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.590
about what people are saying.

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:27.300
Like I, often people ask me, well, have
you ever been in an elevator with Asian

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.430
people and have they said stuff about you?

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:30.090
Never.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:34.230
People don't talk about you, but it's
when you don't understand you conjure

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:38.090
up these ideas that people are talking
about your, what are they saying?

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:41.460
And, and I've often as I'm progressing
in a language when I was learning

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.580
Portuguese I'm with these Brazilians
and they're all talking a mile a

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.630
minute and I feel I should be able
to understand, but I don't understand

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.969
because my brain is not yet used to it.

00:07:51.299 --> 00:07:55.349
And you have to remember that with
enough exposure, you will get better.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:59.280
And so every time you're in a situation
where you don't understand, or you can't

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.380
say what you want to say, that's fine.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:02.190
It's part...

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:03.210
you're on your way.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:07.049
That's part of the process,
uh, of, of getting to know,

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:09.120
you know, acquiring a language.

00:08:09.299 --> 00:08:12.270
And when I was in Machu Picchu,
I was with these other people.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:15.570
And our guide was speaking to me in
different languages and they were a little

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:18.570
bit annoyed that the guide wasn't speaking
in English, they were annoyed that they

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:20.610
couldn't understand what he was saying.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.130
Don't worry about what
you can't understand.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:23.969
That's very important.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:29.550
So begin with a mini stories, reading,
answering questions, where you have

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.580
the answers, where you've actually
heard the answers many, many times

00:08:32.939 --> 00:08:37.409
then gradually move to subjects
which are of greater interest to you.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:42.630
But I would recommend staying with people
who are paid to be sympathetic to you,

00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:44.970
like a tutor or a teacher of some kind.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:49.770
Uh, if you, like I mentioned, when
I was learning Japanese, I had a

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:53.115
very patient assistant, um, Mr.

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:56.625
Nick Yazaki, who was very
long-winded and spoke very,

00:08:56.625 --> 00:08:58.785
you know, humored me and stuff.

00:08:58.905 --> 00:09:01.755
If you have people like that,
fine, but don't put yourself

00:09:01.785 --> 00:09:03.405
in stressful situations.

00:09:03.555 --> 00:09:06.015
Then just keep on speaking.

00:09:06.405 --> 00:09:13.325
Keep on, uh, finding there again, find
a tutor who's good at keeping you going,

00:09:13.385 --> 00:09:17.795
who can ask you questions about things
that you're interested in, where you're

00:09:17.795 --> 00:09:21.845
going to succeed, who doesn't insist
on, you know, like I've had tutors that

00:09:21.875 --> 00:09:23.645
they just, they want to talk about food.

00:09:23.765 --> 00:09:25.865
I don't know the words for
food in let's say Persian.

00:09:26.075 --> 00:09:29.975
I want to talk about history, which
is of interest to me, but my tutor,

00:09:30.035 --> 00:09:31.925
she didn't want to talk about history.

00:09:31.925 --> 00:09:36.100
So get tutors that are sympathetic,
that don't express annoyance when

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:40.810
you make mistakes and who are good at
keeping the conversation going around

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:44.650
things that you are interested in
and where you can express yourself.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:52.910
Then of course, when I get my conversation
report at LingQ, I study it, I listen

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:57.760
to it many times, and I remember the
things that I had trouble saying.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.220
And if I look at a number of these
conversation or parts and listened to

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:06.060
them, I realize that I made the same
mistakes week after week after week.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:07.170
It doesn't matter.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:11.250
You have to have confidence that
you are gradually going to improve.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:17.460
So the answer, how do you get started
speaking and continue to improve?

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:20.340
Reduce the stress, reduce the stress.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:26.475
Start with something like the mini stories
where you're basically reproducing things

00:10:26.475 --> 00:10:28.065
that you have heard many, many times.

00:10:28.245 --> 00:10:32.715
And from there gradually wander
into a greater range of subjects,

00:10:32.745 --> 00:10:36.105
subjects that are of interest to
you, with someone who is sympathetic

00:10:36.105 --> 00:10:40.755
to you and, you know, gradually
your ability to speak will improve.

00:10:40.935 --> 00:10:45.660
And in the end, as I've said, many
times, even though I stress input, to

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:52.310
speak well, you have to speak a lot
so that, uh, I find that I'm speaking

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:56.090
with a tutor once or twice a week, you
know, at the early stages, maybe three

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:58.010
months after I started into the language.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:01.850
But eventually if I want to do well
in the language, I may eventually have

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:07.430
to go to the country, but I also ramp
up, you know, my, my sessions with my

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:09.440
tutor, maybe four or five times a week.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:14.415
So there you have it, my take on
how to get proficient at speaking,

00:11:14.415 --> 00:11:15.944
which is something we all want to do.

00:11:16.214 --> 00:11:19.665
And how do we do it in a
way that minimizes stress.

00:11:20.444 --> 00:11:23.234
And I'll leave you with a
couple of videos that might be

00:11:23.234 --> 00:11:24.584
relevant to you on the subject.

00:11:24.824 --> 00:11:26.265
Okay, then bye for now.

